Adaptations from the pinned source:
- Replace the ARDUINO preprocessor guard name with ESPEAK_EMBEDDED, preserving
  the upstream embedded-asset code paths without any Arduino headers/core.
- Remove the Arduino voice wrapper; retain the original 140-byte English voice.
- Build only synthesis runtime C sources, not file-based dictionary/phoneme
  compilers, asynchronous FIFO workers, desktop audio, MBROLA, Klatt or Sonic.
- Reserve and reuse clause, XML, phoneme and pitch scratch storage. On ESP-IDF,
  the large buffers (including both persistent phoneme-list stages) use PSRAM.
- Check translator/decoder allocations and initial voice-copy enqueue failures.
- Use the NG status-returning API and synchronous retrieval callback. Streaming
  rational linear interpolation converts 22050 Hz mono to 16000 Hz stereo signed
  16-bit output in 256-frame chunks without retaining full utterance PCM.
- Fix an internal SSML parameter call to use the built NG API instead of the
  excluded desktop legacy API wrapper.

Only English (Great Britain) voice and English dictionary are included.
Shared indexed phoneme tables/waveforms and Unicode character data are retained
intact because the English engine references them; other voices/dictionaries,
Arduino wrappers, desktop tools and unrelated audio decoders are not included.
All required synthesis assets are embedded read-only arrays; no runtime data
filesystem, system eSpeak package, Arduino installation, or external speech
service is needed. Call from a single worker with recording disabled and codec
already opened at 16000 Hz, two channels, 16 bits/sample.

Required private compile definitions:
ESPEAK_EMBEDDED=1 USE_ASYNC=0 USE_MBROLA=0 USE_KLATT=0
USE_LIBSONIC=0 USE_LIBPCAUDIO=0
Exact synthesis C source set is explicitly listed in CMakeLists.txt; libm is
required. The IDF dependencies are audio_bsp and heap. The host build has only
standard C/C++ libraries, libm and CMake/compiler dependencies.

Embedded read-only asset footprint (actual array bytes, excluding alignment):
English dictionary 168204; English voice 140; phondata 554740;
phonindex 43316; phontab 58652; intonations 2312. Total: 827364 bytes.
Engine machine code and Unicode tables additionally consume flash; the final
linked IDF map is authoritative for total flash/static RAM consumption.

ESP32 32-bit layout workspace payload: 89650 bytes PSRAM retained and reused
for the engine lifetime (allocator overhead additional). Formula:
804*sizeof(short) + 600*sizeof(WORD_TAB) + 800 +
1000*sizeof(PHONEME_LIST2) + 2001*sizeof(PHONEME_LIST) +
1001*sizeof(SYLLABLE) + 501*sizeof(wchar_t).
The engine's 60ms mono output buffer is 2648 bytes and its event list contains
32 espeak_EVENT entries. Translator/voice/decoder and other small engine state
are additional. Adapter PCM storage is 1024 bytes on the existing chat-worker
stack. There is no full-reply audio allocation and no per-clause scratch churn.
PSRAM allocation failures and codec failures are returned to the caller.

